    "result_text": "All tests green. The full suite (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3/3 passing) confirms the change works and nothing regressed.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented optional `postcode` support for owners, validated per the city's region only when present.\n\n**What the change does**\n- A 4-digit `postcode` may be supplied when creating/updating an owner. When present it must be 4 digits and, for a city with a known region, fall in that region's range (NSW 2000\u20132099, VIC 3000\u20133099, QLD 4000\u20134099). Out-of-range or malformed \u2192 **400**. A city with no known region accepts any 4-digit postcode. When absent, it's optional and the owner is still accepted (backward-compatible). Stored and returned as given.\n\n**Files changed**\n- `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` \u2014 added the optional `postcode` string field (pattern `^[0-9]{4}$`) to the `OwnerFields` schema, so it flows into both the request DTO (`OwnerFieldsDto`) and the response DTO (`OwnerDto`). The pattern gives a free 400 on malformed input via the existing validation handler.\n- `model/Owner.java` \u2014 added the `postcode` field + getter/setter (`@Column(name = \"postcode\")`).\n- `db/{hsqldb,h2,mysql,postgres}/schema.sql` \u2014 added the `postcode` column (tests run on hsqldb with `ddl-auto=none`, so the schema needed it).\n- `rest/function/owner/Locality.java` \u2014 added the region\u2192postcode-range table plus a `postcodeRange(region)` accessor, keeping region logic in one place.\n- `rest/function/owner/CheckOwnerPostcode.java` (new) \u2014 the pipeline step: no-op when absent, else re-checks 4-digit format (robust even for unknown-region cities) then the region range, throwing a checked `InvalidPostcodeException`.\n- `rest/escalation/InvalidPostcodeException.java` + `InvalidPostcodeExceptionHandler.java` (new) + `officefloor/escalation/...InvalidPostcodeException.yml` \u2014 mirror the existing `InvalidEmail` handler, returning 400.\n- `officefloor/rest/api/owners.POST.yml` and `owners/{ownerId}.PUT.yml` \u2014 wired the `checkPostcode` step into both pipelines.\n- `rest/function/owner/ApplyOwner.java` \u2014 copy `postcode` through on PUT.\n\nThe MapStruct `OwnerMapper` maps `postcode` automatically by name (DTO\u2194entity), so storage and the GET response required no mapper change. I left the legacy JDBC repo untouched \u2014 it isn't on the active `spring-data-jpa` test profile and already omits several columns.",
